WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:06.420
The new Wednesday Addams series is a huge hit&nbsp;
and loaded with kooky and spooky easter eggs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:11.400
to the 90s movies and original sitcom!
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.180
and I've been re-watching and pausing&nbsp;
Wednesday to uncover all the weirdest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.620
little details you might have missed!
The series begins with Wednesday going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:24.540
regular school where she finds her brother Pugsley&nbsp;
trussed up with an apple stuffed in his mouth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:30.060
mirroring how he was introduced in the 1991&nbsp;
Addams Family movie where Wednesday had him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:34.260
tied up for archery practice.
Tim Burton directs the new series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:37.500
and there are numerous references&nbsp;
to his movies throughout the show.
&nbsp;

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.940
If you stop and zoom in on Principal&nbsp;
Weem's desk, there's a little dried-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:46.920
head under a glass dome, a nod to Harry The&nbsp;
Hunter's shrunken head from Beetlejuice.
&nbsp;

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:52.680
And the giant bear in the Principal's office&nbsp;
is a call-back to the enormous stuffed bear in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:58.380
the Addams family home from the 60s TV show.
When Wednesday ends up in the infirmary after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:02.070
the gargoyle incident, she lies&nbsp;
in the bed with her arms crossed
&nbsp;

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:05.040
– as if she were in a coffin
– which is also how she went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:09.300
to sleep in the first movie.
After Wednesday meets Tyler for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:14.220
first time, she explains she knows how to fix&nbsp;
his broken coffee machine as she has a device&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.220
with a similar mechanism at home.
Steam-powered guillotine.
&nbsp;

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.160
I built it when I was ten.
I wanted to decapitate my dolls more efficiently.
&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:25.080
This is a throwback to the guillotine scene&nbsp;
in Addams Family Values where Wednesday and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:29.340
Pugsley attempt to re-enact the beheading&nbsp;
of Marie Antoinette on their baby brother. 
&nbsp;

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.740
Death to the enemies of the&nbsp;
people of the Republic.
&nbsp;

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:38.760
And Wednesday's mention of decapitating her dolls&nbsp;
is a nod to both the sitcom where she had a doll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:44.520
with a severed head and also the 90s movie.
The show tips its hat to the classic Addams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.960
Family theme tune from the 60s show in the&nbsp;
first episode when Tyler tells Wednesday…
&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.060
You're not scary.
You're just kinda…
&nbsp;

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.620
kooky.
And she replies…
&nbsp;

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:56.700
I prefer spooky…
The clue to unlocking the secret entrance to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.700
Nightshade Society's basement is to "snap twice"…
[snaps twice]
&nbsp;

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.960
another easter egg to the theme&nbsp;
tune from the original sitcom.
&nbsp;

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:08.880
Wednesday's dancing at the Nevermore Rave'N&nbsp;
has already become a much-loved moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.320
with Jenna Ortega revealing that she&nbsp;
choreographed the whole routine herself!
&nbsp;

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:19.140
She also confirmed my suspicions that some of her&nbsp;
moves were a homage to the dance performed by Lisa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:23.520
Loring in the original sitcom, where "she does [a]&nbsp;
side shuffle with her hands and with her feet."
&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.354
Then if you learn how to dance&nbsp;
well, she'll think you're boss.
&nbsp;

00:02:28.354 --> 00:02:28.370
"Boss"?
Neat...
&nbsp;

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:35.220
Groovy...
Ortega also said she took some inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:40.020
from "The Rich Man's Frug" dance craze that&nbsp;
featured in Bob Fosse's musical Sweet Charity.
&nbsp;

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:45.720
Later at the dance, Lucas and his mates drench&nbsp;
everyone with red paint, in a nod to Stephen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:50.160
King's Carrie where a group of mean girls&nbsp;
shower Carrie with a bucket of pig's blood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.660
something that Wednesday herself references.
They couldn't even spring for real pigs blood.
&nbsp;

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:58.020
It's only paint.
When Xavier asks Wednesday if she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:01.680
ever shot a bow and arrow, she replies…
Only on live targets.
&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:07.020
Which is a call-back to Addams Family Values when&nbsp;
Wednesday and Pugsley were lining up for archery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:11.940
practice and her brother shot down a live bird.
It's an American bald eagle!
&nbsp;

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.040
But aren't they extinct?
They are now.
&nbsp;

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:18.240
Among the many taxidermied mice and&nbsp;
animals at Uriah's Heap, there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:23.340
several easter eggs to Tim Burton's films.
I spotted this one of a dead rodent in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:28.740
wedding dress, a reference to Corpse Bride. 
I also saw this Freddy Krueger creature with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:34.320
the Nightmare on Elm Street villain's iconic&nbsp;
red-striped top, fedora, and blades for hands.
&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:39.960
Like Freddie Krueger who infiltrates and attacks&nbsp;
his victims in their dreams, the Hyde also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:43.740
attacked Xavier during one of his visions.
This creature's been haunting my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.500
dreams for the past couple of weeks.
When I was painting this one, the claws reached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:49.080
out of the canvas and took a swipe at me.
That's how I got these scratches.
&nbsp;

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:54.540
It's Dr Kinbott who creates the dressed-up&nbsp;
taxidermy rodents, and among the many she has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:58.980
at home is one that's dressed as Beetlejuice.
Plus there's another Freddie Krueger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:01.320
one as well. 
When Wednesday goes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:05.520
find Tyler at the Weathervane, she rings&nbsp;
a bell and after he comes out he says 
&nbsp;

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:07.020
[bell dings]
You rang?
&nbsp;

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:12.540
This is a nod to the Addams Family servant Lurch,&nbsp;
and the recurring gag where the family would ring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:17.760
an enormous gong to summon him.
You rang?
&nbsp;

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.520
Yes, Lurch.
And when Tyler wonders whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:24.900
Wednesday was ever a girl scout, she replies…
I can eat Girl Scouts for breakfast.
&nbsp;

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:29.340
This is a funny reference to the Addams&nbsp;
Family movie and Wednesday's reaction when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:33.480
Amanda asked if she or Pugsley wanted&nbsp;
to buy any of her Girl Scout cookies.
&nbsp;

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:37.560
I'll buy a cup if you buy a box of&nbsp;
my delicious Girl Scout cookies.
&nbsp;

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.680
Do we have a deal?
Are they made from real Girl Scouts?
&nbsp;

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:45.540
When Wednesday breaks into the old meeting&nbsp;
house, she comes across an old painting of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:49.380
Crackstone and notices a woman on&nbsp;
the left looking out towards her.
&nbsp;

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:54.900
When the camera cuts in for a close-up, it&nbsp;
appears to be Goody Addams, Wednesday's ancestor.
&nbsp;

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:59.340
However, if you compare this with the&nbsp;
woman in the wide shot of the painting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.360
they aren't the same person.
I can't be sure this isn't a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:08.220
continuity error, but I suspect it could well&nbsp;
be a deliberate decision to give the sense that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:12.600
the painting is alive, or that Goody's ghost&nbsp;
is communicating through it to Wednesday.
&nbsp;

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:16.980
After Wednesday discovers the&nbsp;
truth about Crackstone's evil past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:21.600
she and Thing conspire to burn down&nbsp;
the statue of the evil pilgrim.
&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:26.040
And this feels a lot like a nod to Addams&nbsp;
Family Values where Wednesday instigated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:29.940
a revolt against the camp, and her&nbsp;
gang burned down the pilgrim set.
&nbsp;

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:33.660
I have decided to scalp you and&nbsp;
burn your village to the ground
&nbsp;

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:38.700
By the way, when Crackstone's statue begins&nbsp;
to melt, it's a neat bit of foreshadowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:43.260
for how he burns up in the finale.
Morticia pulling the flower head off a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:48.360
red rose in front of Garrett Gates's grave is&nbsp;
a shout-out to the opening titles of the 60s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:52.860
sitcom and the 90s movie where she cuts&nbsp;
the heads off the roses she's picking.
&nbsp;

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:56.700
And there was another hat-tip to that&nbsp;
movie when we discover via the yearbook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.700 --> 00:06:03.180
that Morticia attended Nevermore in 1991, the&nbsp;
same year The Addams Family movie was released.
&nbsp;

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:09.360
On her way to Nevermore, if you pause, you can see&nbsp;
the suitably-named Addams family license plate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:14.040
which also reveals they live in New Jersey, a&nbsp;
homage to Charles Addams, who drew the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:20.160
cartoons, and was born in the Garden State.
After Gomez is arrested for murder, he has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:27.120
change into prison uniform, which has the number&nbsp;
171912 on it, an easter egg to the date and year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:30.840
that Charles Addams was born;
January 7th, 1912.
&nbsp;

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:36.120
Wednesday wears her trademark black dress and&nbsp;
white collar from her previous incarnations.
&nbsp;

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:41.280
However, at Nevermore she has to wear a&nbsp;
school uniform but only ever dresses in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:45.720
black and white, unlike everyone else. 
And by the end, as she prepares to leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:51.420
Nevermore, she's back in her signature outfit.
Now, Addams Family fans won't have missed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:57.120
painting of Itt in the basement of the Nightshades&nbsp;
Society, however, if you pause here and zoom in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.340
we discover his full name was Ignatius&nbsp;
Itt and he was known as "Supreme Shade".
&nbsp;

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:07.440
Although Ignatius Itt is no longer alive,&nbsp;
the way the show focuses on this painting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:11.820
feels like a hint that we might see one&nbsp;
of his descendants appear in Season 2.
&nbsp;

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:16.200
Principal Weem's costume was&nbsp;
influenced by Tippie Hedren's look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:20.400
in Hitchcock's classic movie The Birds.
And you can see this especially in her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:23.760
overall appearance, including her&nbsp;
hairstyle, at the Harvest Festival.
&nbsp;

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:28.920
It's particularly appropriate given the&nbsp;
overwhelming raven imagery at Nevermore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:33.000
the bird on the Principal's desk, and&nbsp;
also the fact that Wednesday's dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:37.260
foreboding visions place her at the Raven&nbsp;
end of the spectrum of psychic abilities.
&nbsp;

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:41.040
You're a Raven.
Your visions are more potent, more powerful.
&nbsp;

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:46.380
All this avian imagery, including the Rave'n&nbsp;
dance, and the name of the school itself is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:51.480
also a huge shout-out to Edgar Allen Poe's&nbsp;
famous poem, The Raven, whose talking bird&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:56.820
repeatedly says the word 'Nevermore'.
Now, given the homage already to Hitchcock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:01.740
I think Wednesday's ever-so-slight smile&nbsp;
at the very end of the first episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:06.480
is a nod to Norman Bates's infamous&nbsp;
little smile at the end of Psycho.
&nbsp;

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:11.400
When Tyler sets up a movie night date&nbsp;
with Wednesday in Crackstone's Crypt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:16.620
and he puts on Legally Blonde, you can see&nbsp;
her wincing in discomfort from the movie.
&nbsp;

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:21.600
Tyler probably knew she wouldn't like&nbsp;
it, so this could be a subtle red flag&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:26.160
and clue that his intentions towards her&nbsp;
aren't good as we discover by the end.
&nbsp;

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:31.740
And I also think the moment is a little call-back&nbsp;
to Addams Family Values when Gary and Becky force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:36.060
Wednesday, Pugsley and Joel to watch Disney&nbsp;
movies to try and force them to be happy.
&nbsp;

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:38.820
The Little Mermaid.
Stop it.
&nbsp;

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:42.060
He's only a child.
So, I already covered a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:46.500
the clues about why Miss Thornhill would turn&nbsp;
out to be the secret villain in my "Clues You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:51.060
Missed" video, but what I didn't mention was&nbsp;
the one where Thornhill says to the mayor…
&nbsp;

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:54.780
You're always tucked into that little back&nbsp;
booth when I come for my daily matcha latte.
&nbsp;

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:59.520
… but then when she meets Tyler at the Rave'N,&nbsp;
he says she comes in every day for a cappuccino…
&nbsp;

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:02.460
Double-cap, no foam, two&nbsp;
pumps of sugar-free vanilla.
&nbsp;

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:05.520
…and her reply telegraphs the&nbsp;
fact she's up to something.
&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:07.860
It's a small town.
It's hard to keep secrets. 
&nbsp;

